## DESCRIPTION
## exponents
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## BookTitle('Algebra: Form and Function')
## BookChapter('Rules for Exponents and the Reasons for Them')
## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Properties of exponents, rational exponents and radicals)
## DBsection(Properties of exponents)
## Date(01/01/10)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## MLT(Algebra_PropExp_PropExp)
## MLTleader(1)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Algebra: Form and Function')
## AuthorText1('McCallum, Connally, and Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText1('1')
## Section1('6.2')
## Problem1('12')
## KEYWORDS('exponents')

#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$a      = random(5, 9);
$answer = Real("-$a");

#  Main text

BEGIN_PGML
Write the expression as an equivalent expression
in the form [` x^n `].  Simplify your answer as much
as possible, and enter your answer as a fraction.  
  
[``\frac{1}{x^{[$a]}} = x^n ``] for 
[` n = `] [_]{$answer}

[@  helpLink('fractions')  @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Recall the property that 
[``` \frac{1}{x^n} = x^{-n} ```]

Therefore 
[``` \frac{1}{x^{[$a]}} = x^{[$answer]} ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
